    "id": "crashcourse:analysis",
    "title": "Rhetorical and narrative analysis",
    "chapter": "Crashcourse – Advanced Test",
    "prompt": "First analyse how language and first-person narration reveal Leila’s conflict (200–250 words; 25 min). Then compare Leila’s response to public expectations with the officer’s response in Shooting an Elephant (200–250 words; 25 min). Use at least two shared criteria and distinguish the contexts.",
    "fieldGuidance": "Analyse two or three patterns from §§2, 4–7 and the uncertainty in §8.",
    "sourceReference": "Original practice text: The side entrance (§1–8). Cross-text tasks: course-book texts by Orwell and Kureishi.",
    "sources": [
      "practice",
      "orwell",
      "kureishi"
    ],
    "support": "  A paragraph that earns its interpretation   Claim → evidence → technique → explanation → relevance.  A technique is useful only when its function is explained. Analyse the effect on our understanding of Leila’s position, not a vague effect on “the reader”.    Analysis prompts  Possible features: the repeated family metaphor; anaphora in §5; the contrast between official rhetoric and physical work; balanced clauses in §6; the door motif; the ambiguous ending. Show why your chosen feature supports your thesis.    Comparison prompts  Compare fear of losing approval, performance before an audience, choices within unequal structures and retrospective reflection. Leila changes her speech; the officer acts against his judgement. Do not equate the scale or consequences of their actions.    Rhetorical toolkit · precise terms, specific effects   Anaphora  Repeating a beginning across successive clauses. In §5, the repeated “Nobody” turns apparently harmless individual responses into a collective pattern of neglect.  Metaphor  Describing one thing as another. The “family” in §4 implies care and mutual obligation; the neglected entrance tests that promise.  Antithesis / contrast  Setting opposing ideas against each other. Visibility and invisibility in §6 connect public recognition with the erasure of work.  Parallelism  Using repeated grammatical structures. The two “If” clauses in §6 balance different costs of the decision.  Irony  A gap between a stated ideal and the situation presented. The inclusive banner contrasts with the overlooked side entrance.  First-person narration  A narrative choice, not a rhetorical figure. Access to Leila’s doubts encourages closeness while limiting what can be known about other people’s intentions.   "
