    "support": "  Self-assessment · 0–2 per criterion  0 = absent; 1 = partly convincing; 2 = consistent and precise. Score task focus, evidence/accuracy, interpretation/argument, organisation and language separately (10 points per text). This is a practice rubric, not an official exam marking scale. Ask a partner to justify one score.    Advanced Test · 120 minutes    Summary · 20 min:  Outline the conflict between the public message and Leila’s experience (120–150 words).   Analysis · 30 min:  Analyse how rhetoric, contrasts and narrative perspective expose unequal recognition (§§2–8; 250–300 words).   Comparison · 30 min:  Compare the importance of approval and personal conviction for Leila and either Parvez or the officer (250–300 words).   Evaluation · 25 min:  Choose a comment on “Small acts of resistance can change unequal relationships” OR a discussion of whether preserving harmony is more important than challenging authority (200–250 words).   Revision · 15 min:  Check task focus, evidence, paragraph links, tense and grammar.   All lengths are practice targets. The teacher specifies the actual exam duration and requirements.    Review only after writing: possible directions   Possible analytical direction:  celebratory language makes inequality harder to name; the repeated slogan conflicts with the neglected entrance; Leila’s restricted first-person perspective makes approval and shame visible; the ending withholds proof of lasting change. A strong comparison uses a shared question while preserving different power structures. Many well-supported interpretations are possible.  "
